{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/lease.cljc | sed -n '630,750p;950,1035p;1940,2010p' && nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '445,475p;515,550p'\"","aggregated_output":"   630\t                 attribution, called with the source each cursor was wired\n   631\t                 with -- per-author media, an envelope key, or a transport's\n   632\t                 attachment identity. A fact's own claim about its author is\n   633\t                 never consulted\n   634\t  :reclaim       (fn [subject] -> success?), idempotent, reporting; a throw\n   635\t                 counts as \\\"did not report\\\"\n   636\t  :policy        optional (fn [ledger-entry now] -> boolean); truth ends the\n   637\t                 lease with cause :policy. Eligibility precedes policy:\n   638\t                 eligible renewals are applied in the drain whatever the\n   639\t                 policy decides, and a policy may not be implemented by\n   640\t                 declining to count evidence received\n   641\t  :writer        the grantor's stream, where grants and :lapsed are written\n   642\t  :self          the author value the resolver returns for this grantor\n   643\t  :answer        optional (fn [judge drained-proposals] -> {:grants [grant-fact...]\n   644\t                 :refusals [{:proposer author :refusal refusal-fact}...]}),\n   645\t                 called each pass on the proposals it drained (each carrying\n   646\t                 its author); a grantor owes no answer. Answers are keyed by\n   647\t                 the PROPOSER: a grant answers the proposal of the holder it\n   648\t                 carries, while a refusal names no proposer of its own, so\n   649\t                 the hook names the proposal's author it refuses -- one\n   650\t                 holder's answered id never blocks another holder's\n   651\t\n   652\t  Misconfiguration throws here, at assembly. `:seen` and `:answered` grow\n   653\t  with the distinct leases and proposals a judge actually applies --\n   654\t  legitimate growth that only a ledger-persistence design may prune; an\n   655\t  untrusted author's facts never reach either (a forged release registers\n   656\t  nothing, a forged proposal id cannot collide with another author's answer\n   657\t  key). Assembly validation of the medium declarations is the composition\n   658\t  constructor's duty; this builds the state the step threads.\"\n   659\t  [config]\n   660\t  (let [budget (or (get config :drain-budget) default-drain-budget)\n   661\t        units (get config :units default-units)\n   662\t        tolerance (get config :tolerance)\n   663\t        resolver (get config :resolver)]\n   664\t    (check-assembly! (and (integer? budget) (pos? budget))\n   665\t                     \"the drain budget is a positive integer\"\n   666\t                     {:drain-budget budget})\n   667\t    ;; A judge without a working attribution resolver would drop every\n   668\t    ;; lease fact into :dropped -- with the per-element catch in the drain,\n   669\t    ;; silently -- and every lease would then falsely lapse for :silence.\n   670\t    ;; It is an assembly defect, thrown here before any step can run.\n   671\t    (check-assembly! (fn? resolver)\n   672\t                     \"the judge needs an attribution resolver: (fn [source fact] -> author)\"\n   673\t                     {:resolver resolver})\n   674\t    ;; A nil :self would make the resolver's nil answer -- the natural\n   675\t    ;; result for an unknown source or a missing envelope key -- read as\n   676\t    ;; the grantor's own authorship, and a forged :accepted would seed\n   677\t    ;; tenure. Authority needs a non-nil identity to differ from.\n   678\t    (check-assembly! (some? (get config :self))\n   679\t                     \"the judge needs its own author identity: the :self its attribution returns for it, never nil\"\n   680\t                     {:self (get config :self) :refused :self})\n   681\t    (check-units! units)\n   682\t    ;; A tolerance in a unit the table does not know -- or past the unit's\n   683\t    ;; per-unit bound -- would throw from add-duration inside\n   684\t    ;; classification, mid-pass, after step 3 has already delivered grants:\n   685\t    ;; it is an assembly defect, rejected here.\n   686\t    (when (some? tolerance)\n   687\t      (check-assembly! (tolerance? tolerance)\n   688\t                       \"the tolerance is a single-entry {unit non-negative-integer} map within the bound\"\n   689\t                       {:tolerance tolerance :refused :tolerance})\n   690\t      (let [unit (first (keys tolerance))\n   691\t            magnitude (get tolerance unit)]\n   692\t        (check-assembly! (some? (get units unit))\n   693\t                         \"the tolerance's unit is in the unit table\"\n   694\t                         {:tolerance tolerance :units units :refused :tolerance})\n   695\t        (check-assembly! (<= magnitude\n   696\t                             (quot magnitude-limit (get units unit)))\n   697\t                         \"the tolerance's magnitude is within the per-unit bound\"\n   698\t                         {:tolerance tolerance :units units :refused :tolerance})))\n   699\t    {:units units\n   700\t     :tolerance tolerance\n   701\t     :drain-budget budget\n   702\t     :resolver resolver\n   703\t     :reclaim (get config :reclaim)\n   704\t     :policy (get config :policy)\n   705\t     :writer (get config :writer)\n   706\t     :self (get config :self)\n   707\t     :answer (get config :answer)\n   708\t     :ticks []\n   709\t     :facts []\n   710\t     :queue []\n   711\t     :ledger {}\n   712\t     :answered {}\n   713\t     :seen {}\n   714\t     :dropped 0\n   715\t     :drained-proposals []\n   716\t     :now nil\n   717\t     :abort nil\n   718\t     :abort-error nil}))\n   719\t\n   720\t\n   721\t(defn wire-tick\n   722\t  \"Wire one tick cursor: a reader handle, a cursor the composition minted\n   723\t  from the anchor of its choosing, and the `source` the composition binds\n   724\t  to that wiring.\"\n   725\t  [judge handle cursor source]\n   726\t  (update judge :ticks conj {:handle handle\n   727\t                             :cursor cursor\n   728\t                             :retired false\n   729\t                             :source source}))\n   730\t\n   731\t\n   732\t(defn wire-facts\n   733\t  \"Wire one lease-fact cursor over `source` -- the value the composition's\n   734\t  attribution resolver will receive for every fact read here: a per-author\n   735\t  medium, an envelope key's owner, a transport's attachment identity. The\n   736\t  judge learns which leases ride the medium only from the authoritative\n   737\t  facts it observes there, and marks exactly those unknown when that medium\n   738\t  gaps.\"\n   739\t  [judge handle cursor source]\n   740\t  (update judge :facts conj {:handle handle\n   741\t                             :cursor cursor\n   742\t                             :retired false\n   743\t                             :truncated? false\n   744\t                             :leases #{}\n   745\t                             :source source}))\n   746\t\n   747\t\n   748\t(defn author-grant\n   749\t  \"Queue a grant authored outside a pass -- unsolicited, or an answer\n   750\t  composed between passes. Only an `:accepted` fact is authorable; anything\n   950\t  [judge fact]\n   951\t  (assoc-in judge [:ledger (get fact :dao.lease/lease)]\n   952\t            {:subject (get fact :dao.lease/subject)\n   953\t             :holder (get fact :dao.lease/holder)\n   954\t             :duration (get fact :dao.lease/duration)\n   955\t             :max (get fact :dao.lease/max)\n   956\t             :tenure-start (:now judge)\n   957\t             :last-observation (:now judge)\n   958\t             :evidence :known\n   959\t             :released? false\n   960\t             :reclaim {:state :live :cause nil :success nil}}))\n   961\t\n   962\t\n   963\t(defn- apply-valid-fact\n   964\t  \"Apply one structurally valid, admissible lease fact drained from a fact\n   965\t  cursor, stamped with this pass's now. Returns [judge counted-lease-id]:\n   966\t  `counted-lease-id` is the lease this fact was AUTHORITATIVE for -- an\n   967\t  eligible renewal or release from the lease's HOLDER, and nothing else --\n   968\t  and only an authoritative fact registers the lease on the medium it\n   969\t  arrived on. A grantor-authored grant seeds tenure but registers NO\n   970\t  medium: the grant rides the grantor's stream, not the holder's, and\n   971\t  registration is the holder-medium relation the gap and truncation\n   972\t  fallbacks depend on. Everything else establishes nothing and registers\n   973\t  nothing: a holder fact establishes no term (a delayed holder renewal is\n   974\t  still evidence and counts), a renewal or release from any other author\n   975\t  is a fact about that author, a proposal creates no state, a tick on a\n   976\t  fact cursor is noise. An answer is recorded under the PROPOSER it\n   977\t  answers -- for a grant, the holder it carries -- so one holder's answered\n   978\t  proposal id never blocks another's; a drained refusal echo names no\n   979\t  proposer and records no answer.\"\n   980\t  [judge author fact]\n   981\t  (if-some [status (get fact status-key)]\n   982\t    (case status\n   983\t      :dao.lease/proposed\n   984\t      [(update judge :drained-proposals conj {:fact fact :author author}) nil]\n   985\t\n   986\t      :dao.lease/accepted\n   987\t      (if (= author (:self judge))\n   988\t        [(-> judge\n   989\t             (mark-seen (get fact :dao.lease/lease) status)\n   990\t             (mark-answered-maybe (get fact :dao.lease/holder) fact status)\n   991\t             (seed-lease fact))\n   992\t         nil]\n   993\t        [judge nil])\n   994\t\n   995\t      :dao.lease/rejected\n   996\t      (if (= author (:self judge))\n   997\t        ;; An echoed refusal names no proposer: it records no answer key,\n   998\t        ;; and repeat protection for refusals is the hook's delivery gate.\n   999\t        [judge nil]\n  1000\t        [judge nil])\n  1001\t\n  1002\t      :dao.lease/released\n  1003\t      (let [lid (get fact :dao.lease/lease)]\n  1004\t        (if (and (some? (get (:ledger judge) lid))\n  1005\t                 (= author (get-in judge [:ledger lid :holder])))\n  1006\t          [(-> judge\n  1007\t               (mark-seen lid status)\n  1008\t               (assoc-in [:ledger lid :released?] true))\n  1009\t           lid]\n  1010\t          ;; A release from any other author -- or for a lease not in the\n  1011\t          ;; ledger -- establishes nothing, registers nothing, and above all\n  1012\t          ;; does not enter :seen: a forged release must not make the\n  1013\t          ;; holder's own later release read as a repeat.\n  1014\t          [judge nil]))\n  1015\t\n  1016\t      :dao.lease/lapsed\n  1017\t      (if (= author (:self judge))\n  1018\t        [(mark-seen judge (get fact :dao.lease/lease) status) nil]\n  1019\t        [judge nil])\n  1020\t\n  1021\t      [judge nil])\n  1022\t    (case (get fact event-key)\n  1023\t      :dao.lease/renewal\n  1024\t      (let [lid (get fact :dao.lease/lease)]\n  1025\t        (if (and (some? (get (:ledger judge) lid))\n  1026\t                 (= author (get-in judge [:ledger lid :holder])))\n  1027\t          [(-> judge\n  1028\t               (assoc-in [:ledger lid :last-observation] (:now judge))\n  1029\t               (assoc-in [:ledger lid :evidence] :known))\n  1030\t           lid]\n  1031\t          [judge nil]))\n  1032\t      [judge nil])))\n  1033\t\n  1034\t\n  1035\t(defn- on-any-medium\n  1940\t;; creates a stream, installs a timer, holds a callback or reads a clock\n  1941\t;; (C5): the runtime that drives the returned step at the declared\n  1942\t;; cadence is host code outside this namespace, and the reference tick\n  1943\t;; producer lives in the test tree as a stepped deposit function (D3).\n  1944\t\n  1945\t(def medium-retentions\n  1946\t  \"The two retentions a medium may declare: it either evicts its oldest\n  1947\t  values at capacity or retains completely. A medium declared neither\n  1948\t  retaining nor evict-oldest is refused at assembly (C3).\"\n  1949\t  #{:evict-oldest :complete})\n  1950\t\n  1951\t\n  1952\t(def medium-value-domains\n  1953\t  \"The two value domains a medium may declare. `:host-values` declares\n  1954\t  the medium process-local by construction -- its values cannot cross a\n  1955\t  boundary -- which is the declaration's compatibility content beyond\n  1956\t  the enum.\"\n  1957\t  #{:portable-values :host-values})\n  1958\t\n  1959\t\n  1960\t(def attribution-kinds\n  1961\t  \"The three source bindings an attribution can ride on (D4): per-author\n  1962\t  media identity, an envelope key, or a transport's attachment identity.\n  1963\t  A medium declares which one it attributes by; a resolver declares\n  1964\t  which ones it resolves.\"\n  1965\t  #{:per-author-media :envelope-key :attachment-identity})\n  1966\t\n  1967\t\n  1968\t(defn- check-medium-declaration!\n  1969\t  \"C2/C3: validate one wiring's explicit medium declaration -- the\n  1970\t  declaration, not the handle, establishes what the medium is. A\n  1971\t  :complete medium declares no :capacity (nothing evicts); an\n  1972\t  :evict-oldest one declares a positive :capacity. The capacity's\n  1973\t  relation to the judge's cadence is a sizing question (S3: retention\n  1974\t  against facts appended per interval), deliberately NOT derived here\n  1975\t  from the :drain-budget.\"\n  1976\t  [what decl]\n  1977\t  (check-assembly! (map? decl)\n  1978\t                   (str what \" carries an explicit medium declaration map\")\n  1979\t                   {:medium decl :refused :medium})\n  1980\t  (check-assembly! (contains? medium-retentions (get decl :retention))\n  1981\t                   (str what \" declares :retention :evict-oldest or :complete: \"\n  1982\t                        \"a medium declared neither retaining nor evict-oldest \"\n  1983\t                        \"is refused at assembly\")\n  1984\t                   {:medium decl :retention (get decl :retention) :refused :medium})\n  1985\t  (when (= :evict-oldest (get decl :retention))\n  1986\t    (check-assembly! (and (integer? (get decl :capacity))\n  1987\t                          (pos? (get decl :capacity)))\n  1988\t                     (str what \" declares a positive :capacity: it evicts \"\n  1989\t                          \"its oldest values, so it owes the bound it evicts at\")\n  1990\t                     {:medium decl :capacity (get decl :capacity)\n  1991\t                      :refused :medium}))\n  1992\t  (check-assembly! (contains? medium-value-domains (get decl :value-domain))\n  1993\t                   (str what \" declares :value-domain :portable-values or :host-values\")\n  1994\t                   {:medium decl :value-domain (get decl :value-domain)\n  1995\t                    :refused :medium})\n  1996\t  (check-assembly! (contains? attribution-kinds (get decl :attribution))\n  1997\t                   (str what \" declares its :attribution -- one of the three \"\n  1998\t                        \"source bindings: :per-author-media, :envelope-key, \"\n  1999\t                        \":attachment-identity\")\n  2000\t                   {:medium decl :attribution (get decl :attribution)\n  2001\t                    :refused :medium}))\n  2002\t\n  2003\t\n  2004\t(defn- check-cadence!\n  2005\t  \"C1: the composition's runtime drives the step at a declared cadence --\n  2006\t  a maximum interval between COMPLETED passes. A cadence is a strictly\n  2007\t  positive duration in the unit table (zero would demand continuous\n  2008\t  passes), with the tolerance's unit-membership and per-unit checks.\n  2009\t  Runs after `check-units!`: its per-unit division must never meet a\n  2010\t  zero or negative magnitude as a raw host error.\"\n   445\t:dao.lease/lease L}` to the board. Both parties of a call read the board,\n   446\tthen each sends a `descriptor` request with a fresh id to the other's\n   447\treflexive address from the same socket M observed. A path exists when an\n   448\tanswer, even a `not-found` error, carrying one of this peer's outstanding\n   449\tids arrives from the expected address; the first inbound datagram alone\n   450\tconfirms nothing. That is the whole of STUN, as facts on a stream; no probe\n   451\tframe exists.\n   452\t\n   453\t**Bounded meeting work.** M's interpreter bounds its own work: the board's\n   454\tretention (composition data, evict-oldest by default), the number of active\n   455\tpairs in its table, and the fanout of one driver step. A meeting request M\n   456\tcannot honor within its bounds is refused by a gate on its `meet-requests`\n   457\tentry and observed by the asker as `:dao.stream/refused` through its\n   458\treflection: exhaustion is a present answer, not silence. A peer reachable\n   459\tthrough M is reachable only while its channel to M lives, so its keep-alive\n   460\tcadence is bounded by the NAT binding's lifetime, tens of seconds for UDP,\n   461\tlonger for TCP.\n   462\t\n   463\t**Relay, as a convention.** A peer asks M, through `meet-requests`, for a\n   464\tpair; M creates two ring buffers, enters them in its table under a lease,\n   465\tand posts their remote descriptors on the board; the asking peers attach a\n   466\tpair channel over them (3.3). M interprets nothing it relays, and that two\n   467\tunreachable peers need M at all is a fact about the network, not a role in\n   468\tthis protocol. Reflexive addresses on a board are readable by every reader\n   469\tof the board; this convention claims no private delivery.\n   470\t\n   471\t## 5. Conventions that are not protocol\n   472\t\n   473\tNone of these has a frame. Each is peers using sections 2 and 3.\n   474\t\n   475\t**The toy.** Peer B holds a handle `s` over the single value `\"hello\"`,\n   515\t- A table entry served for a remote party (a stream a migrated continuation\n   516\t  still reads): yes; the remote party holds it, the holding peer grants and\n   517\t  judges, the identity is the subject.\n   518\t- A relay pair on a meeting peer: yes; the requesting peer holds it, the\n   519\t  meeting peer grants and judges, the two identities are the subject.\n   520\t- Link state, UDP reassembly buffers, channel sockets: no; governed by close\n   521\t  or by a bounded buffer.\n   522\t- NAT mappings and punch state: no; possessed by the NAT box, so no judge\n   523\t  can exist, and keep-alive cadence is the peer's own (section 4).\n   524\t- Board postings: no; records under the board's retention, each carrying the\n   525\t  lease id of the registration or pair it belongs to, and a reader treats a\n   526\t  posting whose lease has a `:lapsed` on the board as stale.\n   527\t\n   528\t**Mechanism: no new wire shape.** A grantor enters two conventional streams\n   529\tin its table, `lease-proposals` with surface `#{:writer}` and\n   530\t`lease-grants` with surface `#{:reader}`, published as remote descriptors\n   531\tbeside its meeting descriptors. A holder appends a proposal through a\n   532\treflection on the first and reads its grant through a reflection on the\n   533\tsecond. The grant creates one more table entry, a ring buffer with surface\n   534\t`#{:writer}`, and names its identity as `:dao.lease/holder`: this is the\n   535\tholder's **renewal medium**, and whatever lands on it is attributed to the\n   536\tholder by the medium, the attribution resolver `dao.lease.md` names as\n   537\tper-author media. The judge reads it with a local cursor. The renewal\n   538\tmedium lives and dies with its lease; the reclaim procedure removes the\n   539\tsubject entries and the renewal entry together, idempotently, and reports\n   540\tsuccess. A table entry under lease carries `:dao.lease/lease L`.\n   541\t\n   542\tUntil ShiBi exists, a renewal medium is reachable by anyone who learns its\n   543\tdescriptor and accepts a third party's renewal, extending a lease it does\n   544\tnot hold: attribution needs no credential, extension may want one, and a\n   545\tcomposition that must not accept it composes a gate on the renewal entry.\n   546\t\n   547\t**Renewal.** `append!` of `{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal\n   548\t:dao.lease/lease L}` on the holder's reflection of its renewal medium. The\n   549\treflection's `ok` is outbound acceptance only; the holder advances its\n   550\trenewal bound only on the source's `ok` observed on the link's event\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
